  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 314 PM EDT Tue May 12 2026\n\nWednesday Night - Friday...\n\nAs the front from the surface low departs to the east, an amplified \nbut skinny ridge of high pressure builds in over our area, allowing \nfor a brief period of dry weather on Thursday. Temperatures will be \non the cooler side, with highs around 70. For Friday, a few showers \nand a couple of rumbles of thunder are possible (15-20%) ahead of a \nweak cold front from a surface low over Ontario, though any rainfall \nthat does occur will not get much in accumulations. Highs on Friday \nwill be in the upper 70s, preluding what could be one of our warmest \nstretches of the year so far. \n\nSaturday...\n\nA pattern change begins to take place as low-level ridging over the \nSE CONUS builds in. Meanwhile, the previously mentioned cold front \nmoves over the region early Saturday, allowing for a slight chance \n(15-30%) of rain showers during the morning hours. The frontal \nboundary is fairly weak, so temperatures will not cool much, if at \nall, following its passage. Highs are forecast to climb into the mid-\nto-upper 80s, and with prevailing southwesterly flow and decent \nmoisture transport, dew points in the low-to-mid 60s will make for \nsome rather muggy conditions.\n\nA series of perturbations will follow behind the front, moving over \nthe region during the afternoon and evening hours. Given the decent \nmoisture in place (LREF > 1.3\" PW) and very warm temperatures, the \nforcing from the perturbations should allow showers and \nthunderstorms to develop. The most recent LREF guidance gives \ncentral Kentucky and southern Indiana more than 1500 J/kg of SBCAPE, \nthough the vertical wind profile isn't overly impressive. With that \nmuch instability in place, a few stronger storms do have the \npotential to develop, so that'll be something to keep an eye on as \nthe week continues. \n\nSunday - Tuesday... \n\nThe SE CONUS ridge becomes quite amplified over most of the eastern \nCONUS by the end of the day Sunday, with surface high pressure \nsettling in over our area. Dry, hot, and humid weather will close \nout the weekend and start the workweek, with highs in the mid-to-\nupper 80s. A few places could reach 90, with the highest LREF \nprobabilities of temperatures exceeding 90 being around 30-40% near \nBowling Green on Monday. Even if we don't reach 90, dew points in \nthe mid 60s will make for rather sticky outdoor conditions.\n\nA shortwave trough will develop over the eastern Rockies late \nSunday, moving towards the upper Midwest and southern Ontario by \nMonday night. Its cold front will extend southwestward towards the \nsouthern Plains, moving over the Ohio Valley beginning Tuesday \nafternoon, bringing another round of showers and some thunderstorms \nalong with it.",
